			<div class="content" id="post-content-4075470">
									

<p>Thanks for the input. Quite frankly, I wasn't entirely happy with the elements you mentioned at the time of posting. The file evolved as I was writing, and I think the end is much higher quality than the beginning.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Longer&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Longer&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>When you talk about the exposition in the interview, are you referring to when SCP-4051 says the following?</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>SCP-4051:</strong> Sometimes when I use my power and reach into the other place, I can feel others of the same thing that I want to pull out. Like, it’s a space filled with that object.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The idea there was that this was a facet of 4051's character, that he was naturally trusting of authority. He had information that he thought might help, and he shared. Is there any way I can articulate that better?</p>
<p>Also, yes, the bit about not being a number. This was the part I was least satisfied with, but I kept it unchanged because I'm unsure how I'm supposed to avoid talking about it. A reaction like this is logical, if uninteresting. Do you have any pre-existing humanoid SCPs that deal with this issue better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>hand/finger restraints seem like a logical go-to.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, shit, ran this past twenty-odd people and that never occurred to any of us. Thanks.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He's so good and righteous as to not attempt to kill his captors, but attempts escape using a lethal weapon?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Eh, you mean the bomb, the gun, or the knife? Regardless, I tried to point out in the incident report that he'd only use the last two to inflict non-lethal injuries. Is there a way to articulate that better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I lost interest by the conclusion of the Incident Log: seeing another 6 or so collapsibles worth of content I was already having multiple issues with didn't inspire me to tread on.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Entirely understandable, which is why your comment is so important. Thanks a ton for reading as far as you did.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And there's a number of &quot;it's&quot; mistakes that could stand correcting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thanks, I'll work on those. Give me a bit.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Longer&nbsp;Response</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I'm not entirely satisfied with this file as it is now, nor am I really happy with just having an article that doesn't get deleted. It's at +10 at the time of writing, and while that's fine and I'm happy so many people enjoyed it, I'd still like something better.</p>
<p>I'm a bit new around here. Is it uncommon for authors to delete under-performing but positively-rated files to make something better?</p>

							</div>

								
					
